00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.829 align:start position:0%
european union fisheries ministers have  reached agreement on what stocks and how  much fish can be caught next year  by eu vessels in european and other  waters  total allowable catches and fishing  quotas have been set for stocks in the  atlantic  the north sea the baltic and black seas  as well as for international waters  where eu vessels work  the main goal is to avoid overfishing by  managing stocks at maximum sustainable  yields or msy  which is the largest catch that can be  taken from a fish stock  over an indefinite period without  harming it  we have reached three oh 30 stocks  will be fished at msy in 2015.  we have improved our record so for 2014  we are going to bring two stocks more  and for  2015 three more the belief is that  managing stocks according to  maximum sustainable yields will in the  end lead to bigger stocks and a higher  catch  potential recent information published  by the european commission  shows an increase in stocks exploited at  msy levels  and a general decrease in overfishing  the eu's lithuanian presidency said the  catchers and quotas  are in line with the new common  fisheries policy which will come into  force next year  in another major development the council  officially rubber stamped the massive  reform  of the common agricultural policy  kick-starting a new set of measures to  make the cap more competitive and  sustainable  as well as to breathe new life into  europe's rural areas
 

